{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788705246314,"sessionID":"ses_f88dc29b9ffeZI04WaJVvtocST","part":{"id":"prt_077240c68001mFMNOgEKhMrk6V","messageID":"msg_077240a35001kRbwOe1W3orXnp","sessionID":"ses_f88dc29b9ffeZI04WaJVvtocST","snapshot":"1d59abbc7f08872ac99e40bf7e75dfb5d0e17f8d","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788705247636,"sessionID":"ses_f88dc29b9ffeZI04WaJVvtocST","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_27430e4d5d3c409bbcb5fefb","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-erlang-toolchain-empirical-research/plans/erlang-target-research.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-erlang-toolchain-empirical-research/plans/erlang-target-research.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Erlang as a target: what its semantics would actually demand\n2: \n3: Desk research for issue #163, which floats Erlang as a candidate backend whose\n4: point would be to stress test the design. The question here is narrower than \"would\n5: this be nice to have\". The project's admission test for a backend\n6: ([ADR 0002](../docs/adr/0002-backends-as-falsifiers.md), and the research note\n7: [a backend that finds nothing is evidence only if it is different](../research-log/a-backend-that-finds-nothing-is-evidence-only-if-it-is-different.md))\n8: is which axis a candidate is unlike the existing ones on. Everything below is a\n9: documented-semantics comparison; no Erlang toolchain was consulted or run.\n10: \n11: The short version of the finding: Erlang is unlike every existing backend on exactly\n12: one axis, concurrency and message passing, and toylang has no construct that would\n13: exercise that axis. On every axis the language actually uses, Erlang duplicates an\n14: existing witness. As a shipped backend it fails the admission test. As a *thought\n15: experiment* against the open questions, it is the most opinionated thing available,\n16: and two of its features (selective receive, process-as-stream) sit directly on open\n17: design questions. So the stress test is worth doing, but it is a research exercise,\n18: not a backend.\n19: \n20: ## Erlang's model, in the vocabulary the design already uses\n21: \n22: Erlang is a dynamically typed, single-assignment language whose concurrency is the\n23: organizing principle rather than a library. Four facts matter here, each of which is\n24: a claim about toylang vocabulary.\n25: \n26: **Terms are immutable, nested data.** An Erlang term is an atom, integer, float,\n27: binary, tuple, or list, composed arbitrarily. There is no mutation; a variable binds\n28: once per clause and then names a value forever. That is toylang's value model minus\n29: the JSON shapes: an atom is a bare-string enum variant or a string, a tuple is a\n30: record or a single-key enum wrapper, a list is a `Vec`, a binary is a string. The\n31: nested term is exactly the canonical JSON value, rendered in Erlang's own notation.\n32: \n33: **Pattern matching is the central operation, and it is single-assignment.** Erlang\n34: dispatches on shape everywhere: function clauses are tried in order and the first\n35: whose head matches wins, `case` and `receive` do the same. A match binds each\n36: variable at most once, compares literals, and walks nested structure; there is no\n37: backtracking and no unification of one match's bindings with another's. This is\n38: toylang's ordered arm list with the lens-style decode left out.\n39: \n40: **Processes communicate by message passing.** A process has a mailbox. It can\n41: `send` a term to another process's identifier and `receive` messages, and here is\n42: the feature with no toylang analogue: `receive` is *selective*. A process writes a\n43: list of message patterns and the runtime scans the mailbox for the first message\n44: matching any of them; messages that match nothing are left in place for a later\n45: receive. Order of arrival is not order of consumption.\n46: \n47: **There is no type system and no effect system.** Dialyzer infers success typings\n48: as an optional analysis, but the language itself has no compile-time types, no\n49: cardinality, and no notion of an effect. Sending, spawning, and I/O are ordinary\n50: operations with no tracking. A missing clause or a message no pattern matches is a\n51: runtime `function_clause` or `case_clause` error, not a check.\n52: \n53: ## Where toylang maps cleanly\n54: \n55: The mapping is clean exactly where Erlang duplicates a witness the project already\n56: has.\n57: \n58: **Ordered arm matching is the same machinery as Erlang clause matching.** Toylang's\n59: matcher is first-match-wins over an ordered arm list with guards, an `or`\n60: composition, and a `partial` flag that types an honest chain as `Opt`\n61: ([guides/matching](../docs/guides/matching.md), [pattern matching is decoding](../draft.md#pattern-matching-is-decoding)).\n62: Erlang's clause and `receive` matching is the same ordered, single-pass, no-unification\n63: walk. This confirms two decisions rather than challenging them. The no-unification\n64: stance ([Q28](../plans/questions.md#q28-does-deep-matching-need-cross-match-unification-of-logic-variables))\n65: is exactly what Erlang does: `as`-style binding within one arm exists, cross-match\n66: Prolog unification does not. And the `partial` flag is the answer to a hole Erlang\n67: has: toylang types a possibly-total chain instead of deferring to a runtime\n68: `case_clause`. That is toylang doing better, not differently.\n69: \n70: **The value model is a near-translation.** Every toylang value is canonical JSON\n71: and every backend already renders that. Erlang terms are the same nested immutable\n72: data. On the value axis Erlang is another copy of the JSON-native witnesses, with a\n73: different syntax. The enums of [ADR 0009](../docs/adr/0009-enums-are-json-native-single-key-wrappers.md)\n74: lower to Erlang exactly as they lower to the others: a unit variant is an atom, a\n75: payload variant is a tuple of tag plus payload.\n76: \n77: **Immutability is shared, and the cell story stays intact.** Erlang has no mutation\n78: at all. toylang's `cell` is a deliberate, checked exception to an otherwise\n79: immutable model. Erlang is the existence proof that the model works without the\n80: cell, which supports the direction but changes nothing: no toylang program uses\n81: `cell` for anything Erlang would force to be untracked mutable state.\n82: \n83: ## Where it does not map\n84: \n85: **Selective receive is not a stream.** Toylang's effect layer\n86: ([ADR 0001](../docs/adr/0001-stream-is-the-effect-layer-typed.md)) is one linear,\n87: in-order, exactly-once chain: born at a source, consumed once, dead at a sink. An\n88: Erlang mailbox is a holdable, reorderable, partially consumed source. A selective\n89: receive does not consume the front of a queue; it scans a bag for a shape and\n90: leaves the rest. This is the one place Erlang is genuinely unlike everything the\n91: project has built, and it is also the one place toylang has no construct to receive\n92: it. There is no toylang expression that produces a mailbox, and no way to express\n93: \"consume these messages, leave those for later\" in the linear stream model. So\n94: Erlang does not falsify the stream rules; it simply has no analogue to feed into\n95: them.\n96: \n97: **There is no target-level type to be a witness against.** The checked-cardinality\n98: apparatus, `Stream<T>`'s exactly-once rule, `Opt` for partial chains, the enum\n99: exhaustiveness check: none of these has an Erlang counterpart, so none of them gets\n100: stress-tested by emitting to Erlang. A toylang type error that the Go and Rust\n101: backends would catch with a compiler would slip through to a runtime clause error on\n102: Erlang. As a correctness witness Erlang is weaker than Rust, which already provides\n103: the statically-typed, real-sum-type, native-match witness that Erlang would most\n104: resemble.\n105: \n106: **Effects have no spelling to erase.** toylang's effect layer is typed, which is\n107: the point of the streams decision. Erlang's effects (send, spawn, I/O) are\n108: untracked and interleaved freely with pure computation. Emitting a toylang program\n109: to Erlang erases the type-level effect information and replaces it with nothing, so\n110: the thing the streams decision exists to check cannot be observed on this target.\n111: \n112: ## Three candidate framings\n113: \n114: The brief asks how the cardinality/effect model would map onto actor semantics.\n115: Here are the three ways that question can be taken, and what each one concludes.\n116: \n117: **Framing one: Erlang as an eighth backend.** Add `Erlang` to the `Backend` enum and\n118: an `emit_erl.rs` that lowers every feature like the others. This is the framing the\n119: issue most literally proposes, and it is the one that fails the admission test. The\n120: test is which axis a candidate is unlike the existing ones on, and the only axis\n121: Erlang differs on is actors, which toylang programs never use, so the eighth emitter\n122: would never emit a process, a send, or a receive. On value, matching, streaming, and\n123: immutability it duplicates existing witnesses. The cost, an eighth hand-written\n124: emitter and an eighth set of output rules, buys no falsification. Reject.\n125: \n126: **Framing two: Erlang's mailbox as a stress test for the stream model.** Take the\n127: actor's selective receive seriously as a counterexample to the one-way, in-order,\n128: exactly-once stream. This is where Erlang earns its keep, because it sharpens an\n129: open question. The out-of-order consumption is a concrete instance of\n130: [Q4's ordering half](../plans/questions.md#q4-can-the-type-express-ordering-over-heterogeneous-streams),\n131: which the Kleene-pattern algebra of [ADR 0008](../docs/adr/0008-stream-protocols-are-kleene-patterns.md)\n132: deliberately does not cover (its patterns are consumed in arrival order). Erlang shows\n133: what \"consume in a different order than arrival\" costs and why a linear language says\n134: no to it: selective receive is the reason a mailbox can grow without bound and why\n135: unmatched messages can starve. That is a real finding for the design, but it is a\n136: finding about *not* building selective receive, and it does not need Erlang as a\n137: backend to record it.\n138: \n139: **Framing three: Erlang's process-as-stream as a live counterexample to\n140: [ADR 0001](../docs/adr/0001-stream-is-the-effect-layer-typed.md).** The idiomatic\n141: Erlang stream is a process you send messages to; it is a value, a mailbox you can\n142: hold, reorder, and selectively read. ADR 0001 rejected first-class streams as \"the\n143: one irreversible option\", a held value of genuinely unknown extent. Erlang is a\n144: working language built on exactly that held, unknown-extent value, which makes it\n145: the strongest available argument against the rejection. But it is an argument, not a\n146: requirement: Erlang pays for first-class streams with a runtime that scans mailboxes\n147: and a total absence of the checked linearity the streams decision exists to provide.\n148: The tension is worth naming in the ADR's \"considered options\" record, not worth\n149: reopening.\n150: \n151: ## Recommendation\n152: \n153: Do not add Erlang as an eighth backend. Its one distinct axis, concurrency and\n154: message passing, is absent from the toylang language, so an emitter would never\n155: exercise it, and on every exercised axis it duplicates a witness already present\n156: (pattern matching and statically-typed terms duplicate Rust; the JSON value model\n157: duplicates Lua, JS, and Python; the streaming loop duplicates jq and the others).\n158: By the project's own admission test, which the research log states as \"not whether\n159: it would be useful to have, but which axis it is unlike the others on\", Erlang fails.\n160: \n161: Do use Erlang as a design stress test in the cheaper sense: a documented comparison\n162: against the open questions it is most opinionated about. It is the best available\n163: probe for two open items. It gives [Q4](../plans/questions.md#q4-can-the-type-express-ordering-over-heterogeneous-streams)\n164: a concrete picture of out-of-order consumption and why the Kleene-pattern shape\n165: declines it, and it gives [Q35](../plans/questions.md#q35-what-are-stdout-and-stderr-and-does-a-program-write-or-return)\n166: the strongest version of the \"output is an effect, not a value\" answer, since Erlang\n167: has many processes each writing independently and no single program value at all.\n168: \n169: If the language ever grows concurrency, revisit Erlang as a backend: it would then\n170: be the obvious first witness on a new axis, the way jq was the first witness on\n171: generative streaming. Until then, the honest summary is that Erlang is a question\n172: the design should answer in prose, not a target it should compile to.\n173: \n174: If issue #163 is to be closed rather than left as a backlog idea, the recommendation\n175: is to record this analysis and close it as \"considered, not a backend, see\n176: plans/erlang-target-research.md\". The one condition that would reopen it, added\n177: concurrency, is not on any board.\n\n(End of file - total 177 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Erlang as a target: what its semantics would actually demand\n\nDesk research for issue #163, which floats Erlang as a candidate backend whose\npoint would be to stress test the design. The question here is narrower than \"would\nthis be nice to have\". The project's admission test for a backend\n([ADR 0002](../docs/adr/0002-backends-as-falsifiers.md), and the research note\n[a backend that finds nothing is evidence only if it is different](../research-log/a-backend-that-finds-nothing-is-evidence-only-if-it-is-different.md))\nis which axis a candidate is unlike the existing ones on. Everything below is a\ndocumented-semantics comparison; no Erlang toolchain was consulted or run.\n\nThe short version of the finding: Erlang is unlike every existing backend on exactly\none axis, concurrency and message passing, and toylang has no construct that would\nexercise that axis. On every axis the language actually uses, Erlang duplicates an\nexisting witness. As a shipped backend it fails the admission test. As a *thought\nexperiment* against the open questions, it is the most opinionated thing available,\nand two of its features (selective receive, process-as-stream) sit directly on open\ndesign questions. So the stress test is worth doing, but it is a research exercise,\nnot a backend.\n\n## Erlang's model, in the vocabulary the design already uses","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-erlang-toolchain-empirical-research/plans/erlang-target-research.md","text":"# Erlang as a target: what its semantics would actually demand\n\nDesk research for issue #163, which floats Erlang as a candidate backend whose\npoint would be to stress test the design. The question here is narrower than \"would\nthis be nice to have\". The project's admission test for a backend\n([ADR 0002](../docs/adr/0002-backends-as-falsifiers.md), and the research note\n[a backend that finds nothing is evidence only if it is different](../research-log/a-backend-that-finds-nothing-is-evidence-only-if-it-is-different.md))\nis which axis a candidate is unlike the existing ones on. Everything below is a\ndocumented-semantics comparison; no Erlang toolchain was consulted or run.\n\nThe short version of the finding: Erlang is unlike every existing backend on exactly\none axis, concurrency and message passing, and toylang has no construct that would\nexercise that axis. On every axis the language actually uses, Erlang duplicates an\nexisting witness. As a shipped backend it fails the admission test. As a *thought\nexperiment* against the open questions, it is the most opinionated thing available,\nand two of its features (selective receive, process-as-stream) sit directly on open\ndesign questions. So the stress test is worth doing, but it is a research exercise,\nnot a backend.\n\n## Erlang's model, in the vocabulary the design already uses\n\nErlang is a dynamically typed, single-assignment language whose concurrency is the\norganizing principle rather than a library. Four facts matter here, each of which is\na claim about toylang vocabulary.\n\n**Terms are immutable, nested data.** An Erlang term is an atom, integer, float,\nbinary, tuple, or list, composed arbitrarily. There is no mutation; a variable binds\nonce per clause and then names a value forever. That is toylang's value model minus\nthe JSON shapes: an atom is a bare-string enum variant or a string, a tuple is a\nrecord or a single-key enum wrapper, a list is a `Vec`, a binary is a string. The\nnested term is exactly the canonical JSON value, rendered in Erlang's own notation.\n\n**Pattern matching is the central operation, and it is single-assignment.** Erlang\ndispatches on shape everywhere: function clauses are tried in order and the first\nwhose head matches wins, `case` and `receive` do the same. A match binds each\nvariable at most once, compares literals, and walks nested structure; there is no\nbacktracking and no unification of one match's bindings with another's. This is\ntoylang's ordered arm list with the lens-style decode left out.\n\n**Processes communicate by message passing.** A process has a mailbox. It can\n`send` a term to another process's identifier and `receive` messages, and here is\nthe feature with no toylang analogue: `receive` is *selective*. A process writes a\nlist of message patterns and the runtime scans the mailbox for the first message\nmatching any of them; messages that match nothing are left in place for a later\nreceive. Order of arrival is not order of consumption.\n\n**There is no type system and no effect system.** Dialyzer infers success typings\nas an optional analysis, but the language itself has no compile-time types, no\ncardinality, and no notion of an effect. Sending, spawning, and I/O are ordinary\noperations with no tracking. A missing clause or a message no pattern matches is a\nruntime `function_clause` or `case_clause` error, not a check.\n\n## Where toylang maps cleanly\n\nThe mapping is clean exactly where Erlang duplicates a witness the project already\nhas.\n\n**Ordered arm matching is the same machinery as Erlang clause matching.** Toylang's\nmatcher is first-match-wins over an ordered arm list with guards, an `or`\ncomposition, and a `partial` flag that types an honest chain as `Opt`\n([guides/matching](../docs/guides/matching.md), [pattern matching is decoding](../draft.md#pattern-matching-is-decoding)).\nErlang's clause and `receive` matching is the same ordered, single-pass, no-unification\nwalk. This confirms two decisions rather than challenging them. The no-unification\nstance ([Q28](../plans/questions.md#q28-does-deep-matching-need-cross-match-unification-of-logic-variables))\nis exactly what Erlang does: `as`-style binding within one arm exists, cross-match\nProlog unification does not. And the `partial` flag is the answer to a hole Erlang\nhas: toylang types a possibly-total chain instead of deferring to a runtime\n`case_clause`. That is toylang doing better, not differently.\n\n**The value model is a near-translation.** Every toylang value is canonical JSON\nand every backend already renders that. Erlang terms are the same nested immutable\ndata. On the value axis Erlang is another copy of the JSON-native witnesses, with a\ndifferent syntax. The enums of [ADR 0009](../docs/adr/0009-enums-are-json-native-single-key-wrappers.md)\nlower to Erlang exactly as they lower to the others: a unit variant is an atom, a\npayload variant is a tuple of tag plus payload.\n\n**Immutability is shared, and the cell story stays intact.** Erlang has no mutation\nat all. toylang's `cell` is a deliberate, checked exception to an otherwise\nimmutable model. Erlang is the existence proof that the model works without the\ncell, which supports the direction but changes nothing: no toylang program uses\n`cell` for anything Erlang would force to be untracked mutable state.\n\n## Where it does not map\n\n**Selective receive is not a stream.** Toylang's effect layer\n([ADR 0001](../docs/adr/0001-stream-is-the-effect-layer-typed.md)) is one linear,\nin-order, exactly-once chain: born at a source, consumed once, dead at a sink. An\nErlang mailbox is a holdable, reorderable, partially consumed source. A selective\nreceive does not consume the front of a queue; it scans a bag for a shape and\nleaves the rest. This is the one place Erlang is genuinely unlike everything the\nproject has built, and it is also the one place toylang has no construct to receive\nit. There is no toylang expression that produces a mailbox, and no way to express\n\"consume these messages, leave those for later\" in the linear stream model. So\nErlang does not falsify the stream rules; it simply has no analogue to feed into\nthem.\n\n**There is no target-level type to be a witness against.** The checked-cardinality\napparatus, `Stream<T>`'s exactly-once rule, `Opt` for partial chains, the enum\nexhaustiveness check: none of these has an Erlang counterpart, so none of them gets\nstress-tested by emitting to Erlang. A toylang type error that the Go and Rust\nbackends would catch with a compiler would slip through to a runtime clause error on\nErlang. As a correctness witness Erlang is weaker than Rust, which already provides\nthe statically-typed, real-sum-type, native-match witness that Erlang would most\nresemble.\n\n**Effects have no spelling to erase.** toylang's effect layer is typed, which is\nthe point of the streams decision. Erlang's effects (send, spawn, I/O) are\nuntracked and interleaved freely with pure computation. Emitting a toylang program\nto Erlang erases the type-level effect information and replaces it with nothing, so\nthe thing the streams decision exists to check cannot be observed on this target.\n\n## Three candidate framings\n\nThe brief asks how the cardinality/effect model would map onto actor semantics.\nHere are the three ways that question can be taken, and what each one concludes.\n\n**Framing one: Erlang as an eighth backend.** Add `Erlang` to the `Backend` enum and\nan `emit_erl.rs` that lowers every feature like the others. This is the framing the\nissue most literally proposes, and it is the one that fails the admission test. The\ntest is which axis a candidate is unlike the existing ones on, and the only axis\nErlang differs on is actors, which toylang programs never use, so the eighth emitter\nwould never emit a process, a send, or a receive. On value, matching, streaming, and\nimmutability it duplicates existing witnesses. The cost, an eighth hand-written\nemitter and an eighth set of output rules, buys no falsification. Reject.\n\n**Framing two: Erlang's mailbox as a stress test for the stream model.** Take the\nactor's selective receive seriously as a counterexample to the one-way, in-order,\nexactly-once stream. This is where Erlang earns its keep, because it sharpens an\nopen question. The out-of-order consumption is a concrete instance of\n[Q4's ordering half](../plans/questions.md#q4-can-the-type-express-ordering-over-heterogeneous-streams),\nwhich the Kleene-pattern algebra of [ADR 0008](../docs/adr/0008-stream-protocols-are-kleene-patterns.md)\ndeliberately does not cover (its patterns are consumed in arrival order). Erlang shows\nwhat \"consume in a different order than arrival\" costs and why a linear language says\nno to it: selective receive is the reason a mailbox can grow without bound and why\nunmatched messages can starve. That is a real finding for the design, but it is a\nfinding about *not* building selective receive, and it does not need Erlang as a\nbackend to record it.\n\n**Framing three: Erlang's process-as-stream as a live counterexample to\n[ADR 0001](../docs/adr/0001-stream-is-the-effect-layer-typed.md).** The idiomatic\nErlang stream is a process you send messages to; it is a value, a mailbox you can\nhold, reorder, and selectively read. ADR 0001 rejected first-class streams as \"the\none irreversible option\", a held value of genuinely unknown extent. Erlang is a\nworking language built on exactly that held, unknown-extent value, which makes it\nthe strongest available argument against the rejection. But it is an argument, not a\nrequirement: Erlang pays for first-class streams with a runtime that scans mailboxes\nand a total absence of the checked linearity the streams decision exists to provide.\nThe tension is worth naming in the ADR's \"considered options\" record, not worth\nreopening.\n\n## Recommendation\n\nDo not add Erlang as an eighth backend. Its one distinct axis, concurrency and\nmessage passing, is absent from the toylang language, so an emitter would never\nexercise it, and on every exercised axis it duplicates a witness already present\n(pattern matching and statically-typed terms duplicate Rust; the JSON value model\nduplicates Lua, JS, and Python; the streaming loop duplicates jq and the others).\nBy the project's own admission test, which the research log states as \"not whether\nit would be useful to have, but which axis it is unlike the others on\", Erlang fails.\n\nDo use Erlang as a design stress test in the cheaper sense: a documented comparison\nagainst the open questions it is most opinionated about. It is the best available\nprobe for two open items. It gives [Q4](../plans/questions.md#q4-can-the-type-express-ordering-over-heterogeneous-streams)\na concrete picture of out-of-order consumption and why the Kleene-pattern shape\ndeclines it, and it gives [Q35](../plans/questions.md#q35-what-are-stdout-and-stderr-and-does-a-program-write-or-return)\nthe strongest version of the \"output is an effect, not a value\" answer, since Erlang\nhas many processes each writing independently and no single program value at all.\n\nIf the language ever grows concurrency, revisit Erlang as a backend: it would then\nbe the obvious first witness on a new axis, the way jq was the first witness on\ngenerative streaming. Until then, the honest summary is that Erlang is a question\nthe design should answer in prose, not a target it should compile to.\n\nIf issue #163 is to be closed rather than left as a backlog idea, the recommendation\nis to record this analysis and close it as \"considered, not a backend, see\nplans/erlang-target-research.md\". The one condition that would reopen it, added\nconcurrency, is not on any board.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":177,"totalLines":177,"truncated":false}},"title":"plans/erlang-target-research.md","time":{"start":1788705247628,"end":1788705247634}},"id":"prt_077241188001XM4bMlE0h3xd8Z","sessionID":"ses_f88dc29b9ffeZI04WaJVvtocST","messageID":"msg_077240a35001kRbwOe1W3orXnp"}}
