{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788705251140,"sessionID":"ses_f88dc29b9ffeZI04WaJVvtocST","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_5a31ba41a7164d6099c882df","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"rg -n \"erlang|170|163\" plans/board.yaml plans/board-archive.yaml 2>&1 | head -40"},"output":"plans/board-archive.yaml:1252:- id: erlang-target-research\nplans/board-archive.yaml:1253:  issue: gh:163\nplans/board-archive.yaml:1254:  title: 'Research spike (gh:163''s own issue body flags this as needing investigation first): survey Erlang''s process/effect model, pattern matching, and immutability against toylang''s current design docs and its seven existing backends (Lua, JS, Native/LLVM, jq, Go, Python, Rust) -- identify what a design stress test would actually surface. Write findings to plans/. Run 1 (2026-08-31) did 18 legit research steps then tried to verify an Erlang toolchain (`which erl`/`erl -eval`), got denied, and gave up with zero output -- likely provoked by the standard brief wrapper''s \"do not touch plans/\" line contradicting this task''s own deliverable. Rebriefed 2026-09-01 with BRIEF_RAW=1 (no wrapper, explicit \"no toolchain needed, write plans/erlang-target-research.md\"); incident logged in plans/opencode-rollout.md. The lane''s `ahead=7` is entirely inherited base content from the to-merge-1788204752 accumulator (dispatch base at the time), not this task''s own work -- do not read `ahead>0` on this lane as landable without checking for a real new commit past that base.'\nplans/board-archive.yaml:1270:  title: 'Consider running other modules as functions, e.g. matcher arms dispatching to a submodule file (maintainer note) -- ruled (module-routing-and-erlang-target round, question module-as-function-routing-design): option C, `@path` dedicated routing-arm form, chosen over A ($(path) call-shaped) and B (path::fn named-function). Maintainer refined the spelling to parenthesized `@(route-groups/baz.toy)`. Spins off file-visibility-tracking-build (prerequisite, per the round''s own thesis that the checker doesn''t yet track per-file definition origin) and module-routing-syntax-build.'\nplans/board-archive.yaml:1277:  title: 'Checker tracks which file a definition came from and enforces per-call-site visibility -- prerequisite named by the module-as-function-routing-design ruling (module-routing-and-erlang-target round): the same gap that already blocks a `pub` prelude function from calling a private one blocks any module-dispatch syntax from meaning anything.'\nplans/board-archive.yaml:1328:- id: stuck-issue-170-investigation\nplans/board-archive.yaml:1332:  issue: gh:170\nplans/board-archive.yaml:1333:  title: 'INVESTIGATE stuck lane issue-170 (no activity 22h, 1 run(s), 0 commits): evidence frozen in plans/incidents/issue-170-20260901/ -- read it plus the lane worktree, then report in plans/opencode-rollout.md whether this was brief clarity, a capability gap, a tooling/permission trap, or task shape, and propose the rebrief or reshape. Do NOT attempt the original task.'\nplans/board.yaml:204:  title: 'Implement `@(path)` module-as-function routing-arm syntax -- ruled (module-routing-and-erlang-target round, question module-as-function-routing-design): option C (dedicated routing-arm form) chosen; maintainer refined the spelling from the round''s bare `@path` to parenthesized `@(route-groups/baz.toy)` because it encloses the path expression. See plans/module-routing-research.md.'\nplans/board.yaml:210:- id: erlang-target-consideration\nplans/board.yaml:211:  issue: gh:163\nplans/board.yaml:212:  title: 'Consider Erlang as an additional backend target, as a design stress test (maintainer note) -- round answer (module-routing-and-erlang-target round, question erlang-target-consideration): option C, spend more research and actually try the Erlang toolchain rather than accepting the desk-research-only finding (plans/erlang-target-research.md). Maintainer also flagged: concurrency will need to be supported by toylang regardless of this backend question -- worth surfacing as its own open item once this research lands.'\nplans/board.yaml:214:  needs: [erlang-toolchain-empirical-research]\nplans/board.yaml:218:- id: erlang-toolchain-empirical-research\nplans/board.yaml:219:  issue: gh:170\nplans/board.yaml:220:  title: 'Research spike (round answer, 2026-09-01; filed as gh:170, a follow-up to gh:163 since the original issue-163 branch is already merged/archived): go beyond plans/erlang-target-research.md''s desk research -- actually install/verify the Erlang toolchain and run the documented-semantics comparison (process/effect model, selective receive, pattern matching) against real `erl`/`erlc` output, not just written docs. Prior attempt (erlang-target-research run 1) hit a sandbox denial trying `which erl`/`erl -eval` and gave up; that incident is logged in plans/opencode-rollout.md -- this is an infrastructure-level restriction, so brief this run to document the denial precisely and stop rather than retry through another channel if it recurs. Write findings as an addendum to plans/erlang-target-research.md.'\n","metadata":{"output":"plans/board-archive.yaml:1252:- id: erlang-target-research\nplans/board-archive.yaml:1253:  issue: gh:163\nplans/board-archive.yaml:1254:  title: 'Research spike (gh:163''s own issue body flags this as needing investigation first): survey Erlang''s process/effect model, pattern matching, and immutability against toylang''s current design docs and its seven existing backends (Lua, JS, Native/LLVM, jq, Go, Python, Rust) -- identify what a design stress test would actually surface. Write findings to plans/. Run 1 (2026-08-31) did 18 legit research steps then tried to verify an Erlang toolchain (`which erl`/`erl -eval`), got denied, and gave up with zero output -- likely provoked by the standard brief wrapper''s \"do not touch plans/\" line contradicting this task''s own deliverable. Rebriefed 2026-09-01 with BRIEF_RAW=1 (no wrapper, explicit \"no toolchain needed, write plans/erlang-target-research.md\"); incident logged in plans/opencode-rollout.md. The lane''s `ahead=7` is entirely inherited base content from the to-merge-1788204752 accumulator (dispatch base at the time), not this task''s own work -- do not read `ahead>0` on this lane as landable without checking for a real new commit past that base.'\nplans/board-archive.yaml:1270:  title: 'Consider running other modules as functions, e.g. matcher arms dispatching to a submodule file (maintainer note) -- ruled (module-routing-and-erlang-target round, question module-as-function-routing-design): option C, `@path` dedicated routing-arm form, chosen over A ($(path) call-shaped) and B (path::fn named-function). Maintainer refined the spelling to parenthesized `@(route-groups/baz.toy)`. Spins off file-visibility-tracking-build (prerequisite, per the round''s own thesis that the checker doesn''t yet track per-file definition origin) and module-routing-syntax-build.'\nplans/board-archive.yaml:1277:  title: 'Checker tracks which file a definition came from and enforces per-call-site visibility -- prerequisite named by the module-as-function-routing-design ruling (module-routing-and-erlang-target round): the same gap that already blocks a `pub` prelude function from calling a private one blocks any module-dispatch syntax from meaning anything.'\nplans/board-archive.yaml:1328:- id: stuck-issue-170-investigation\nplans/board-archive.yaml:1332:  issue: gh:170\nplans/board-archive.yaml:1333:  title: 'INVESTIGATE stuck lane issue-170 (no activity 22h, 1 run(s), 0 commits): evidence frozen in plans/incidents/issue-170-20260901/ -- read it plus the lane worktree, then report in plans/opencode-rollout.md whether this was brief clarity, a capability gap, a tooling/permission trap, or task shape, and propose the rebrief or reshape. Do NOT attempt the original task.'\nplans/board.yaml:204:  title: 'Implement `@(path)` module-as-function routing-arm syntax -- ruled (module-routing-and-erlang-target round, question module-as-function-routing-design): option C (dedicated routing-arm form) chosen; maintainer refined the spelling from the round''s bare `@path` to parenthesized `@(route-groups/baz.toy)` because it encloses the path expression. See plans/module-routing-research.md.'\nplans/board.yaml:210:- id: erlang-target-consideration\nplans/board.yaml:211:  issue: gh:163\nplans/board.yaml:212:  title: 'Consider Erlang as an additional backend target, as a design stress test (maintainer note) -- round answer (module-routing-and-erlang-target round, question erlang-target-consideration): option C, spend more research and actually try the Erlang toolchain rather than accepting the desk-research-only finding (plans/erlang-target-research.md). Maintainer also flagged: concurrency will need to be supported by toylang regardless of this backend question -- worth surfacing as its own open item once this research lands.'\nplans/board.yaml:214:  needs: [erlang-toolchain-empirical-research]\nplans/board.yaml:218:- id: erlang-toolchain-empirical-research\nplans/board.yaml:219:  issue: gh:170\nplans/board.yaml:220:  title: 'Research spike (round answer, 2026-09-01; filed as gh:170, a follow-up to gh:163 since the original issue-163 branch is already merged/archived): go beyond plans/erlang-target-research.md''s desk research -- actually install/verify the Erlang toolchain and run the documented-semantics comparison (process/effect model, selective receive, pattern matching) against real `erl`/`erlc` output, not just written docs. Prior attempt (erlang-target-research run 1) hit a sandbox denial trying `which erl`/`erl -eval` and gave up; that incident is logged in plans/opencode-rollout.md -- this is an infrastructure-level restriction, so brief this run to document the denial precisely and stop rather than retry through another channel if it recurs. Write findings as an addendum to plans/erlang-target-research.md.'\n","exit":0,"trunca