{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788705258951,"sessionID":"ses_f88dc29b9ffeZI04WaJVvtocST","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_bf20280ce427446098946036","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"ls -la plans/incidents/issue-170-20260901/ 2>&1; echo \"---\"; ls -la plans/incidents/ 2>&1 | head -20"},"output":"total 128\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord    108 Sep  6 16:33 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord   1486 Sep  6 16:33 ..\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord 123717 Sep  6 16:33 20260901-010824-issue-170.jsonl.tail\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord    214 Sep  6 16:33 worktree-state.txt\n---\ntotal 0\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord 1486 Sep  6 16:33 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord 2130 Sep  6 16:33 ..\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord  108 Sep  6 16:33 issue-140-20260901\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord  180 Sep  6 16:33 issue-149-20260902\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord  180 Sep  6 16:33 issue-152-20260902\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord  180 Sep  6 16:33 issue-152-20260903\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord  180 Sep  6 16:33 issue-153-20260902\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord  180 Sep  6 16:33 issue-158-20260902\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord  180 Sep  6 16:33 issue-159-20260903\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord  180 Sep  6 16:33 issue-168-20260902\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord  108 Sep  6 16:33 issue-169-20260901\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord  108 Sep  6 16:33 issue-170-20260901\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord  108 Sep  6 16:33 issue-172-20260902\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord  108 Sep  6 16:33 issue-174-20260902\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord  180 Sep  6 16:33 issue-177-20260903\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord  162 Sep  6 16:33 issue-benchmark-fannkuch-redux-build-20260906\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord  144 Sep  6 16:33 issue-benchmark-fasta-build-20260906\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord  130 Sep  6 16:33 issue-float-build-go-20260904\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord  228 Sep  6 16:33 issue-float-build-lua-20260904\n","metadata":{"output":"total 128\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord    108 Sep  6 16:33 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord   1486 Sep  6 16:33 ..\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord 123717 Sep  6 16:33 20260901-010824-issue-170.jsonl.tail\n-rw-r--r-- 1 kantord kantord    214 Sep  6 16:33 worktree-state.txt\n---\ntotal 0\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord 1486 Sep  6 16:33 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord 2130 Sep  6 16:33 ..\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord  108 Sep  6 16:33 issue-140-20260901\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord  180 Sep  6 16:33 issue-149-20260902\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord  180 Sep  6 16:33 issue-152-20260902\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord  180 Sep  6 16:33 issue-152-20260903\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord  180 Sep  6 16:33 issue-153-20260902\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord  180 Sep  6 16:33 issue-158-20260902\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord  180 Sep  6 16:33 issue-159-20260903\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord  180 Sep  6 16:33 issue-168-20260902\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord  108 Sep  6 16:33 issue-169-20260901\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord  108 Sep  6 16:33 issue-170-20260901\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord  108 Sep  6 16:33 issue-172-20260902\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord  108 Sep  6 16:33 issue-174-20260902\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord  180 Sep  6 16:33 issue-177-20260903\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord  162 Sep  6 16:33 issue-benchmark-fannkuch-redux-build-20260906\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord  144 Sep  6 16:33 issue-benchmark-fasta-build-20260906\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord  130 Sep  6 16:33 issue-float-build-go-20260904\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 kantord kantord  228 Sep  6 16:33 issue-float-build-lua-20260904\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"ls -la plans/incidents/issue-170-20260901/ 2>&1; echo \"---\"; ls -la plans/incidents/ 2>&1 | head -20","time":{"start":1788705258946,"end":1788705258949}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I understand the full context. Let me read the investigation record in plans/incidents/issue-170-20260901/ and the opencode-rollout incident sections. Also the board.yaml around line 210-220.\n\nKey facts:\n- The prior runs tried `which erl erlc escript erl_call` -- that compound was denied, but `which` alone is allowed (found nothing on PATH).\n- Running `erl` is denied by the permission allow-list.\n\nSo the situation: the task is to empirically verify the Erlang toolchain. But: `erl` execution is denied, `which` alone is allowed. The prior investigation (incident) concluded: \"the empirical verification is impossible on this host: erl is denied by the permission allow-list; installation (apt/snap/network) is also denied\"...\n\nWait — but my brief says: \"Prior attempt hit a sandbox denial trying 'which erl'/'erl -eval' and gave up (logged in plans/opencode-rollout.md) -- this is an infrastructure-level restriction: if it recurs, document the denial precisely and stop, do not retry through another channel.\"\n\nAnd hard constraints say: \"do NOT touch plans/\". Wait, but the deliverable says \"Write findings as an addendum to plans/erlang-target-research.md\". Hmm. The hard constraints say \"do NOT touch plans/\". But the task says write an addendum to plans/erlang-target-research.md. Contradiction?\n\nLet me re-read. \"Hard constraints: work ONLY inside this worktree; do NOT push; do not touch plans/.\"\n\nBut the task says \"Write findings as an addendum to plans/erlang-target-research.md.\"\n\nHmm. That's contradictory. Unless... the \"do not touch plans/\" refers to not modifying the plan documents in a way that conflicts, or the addendum is the deliverable and plans/ is where it lives. Let me look at what the incident investigation said. The board-archive says run 1 \"likely provoked by the standard brief wrapper's 'do not touch plans/' line contradicting this task's own deliverable.\" So this contradiction was already identified as the cause of the first failure. The rebrief (BRIEF_RAW=1, no wrapper) fixed it for gh:163.\n\nNow my brief is the enwiro-delegate brief which includes \"do not touch plans/\". But the task says write findings as an addendum to plans/erlang-target-research.md. So the \"do not touch plans/\" must be understood as not touching OTHER plans files, or it's a template artifact. Given the incident explicitly flagged this contradiction as the cause of failure, I should interpret \"do not touch plans/\" narrowly: the deliverable file plans/erlang-target-research.md is the exception (the task explicitly names it as the write target). Actually, re-reading my brief: \"Write findings as an addendum to plans/erlang-target-research.md.\" — that's an explicit instruction. And hard constraints: \"do not touch plans/.\" \n\nHmm. Which wins? The investigation in opencode-rollout.md line 319-325 (the rebrief decision tree) says:\n\n\"(b) toolchain absent -> append an addendum to plans/erlang-target-research.md documenting: no binaries on PATH or in /usr/bin, /usr/local/bin, /opt, /snap, /usr/lib/erlang;executing erl is additionally denied...\"\n\nSo the intended deliverable IS writing an addendum to plans/erlang-target-research.md. The \"do not touch plans/\" constraint in my brief conflicts with that. But the incident investigation explicitly identified that conflict as the cause of run 1 failure. Given the explicit deliverable instruction, and the incident record's own rebrief fixing it, I'll treat plans/erlang-target-research.md as the one allowed write target. Let me read the incident record fully to be sure.\n\nand check the ESCALATION.md existence.\n\nLet me read plans/incidents/issue-170-20260901/ and the relevant opencode-rollout sections.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_077243d90001cnzE8z0dg42PtS","sessionID":"ses_f88dc29b9ffeZI04WaJVvtocST","messageID":"msg_077241f6e001h55pBKir28HncB"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788705258956,"sessionID":"ses_f88dc29b9ffeZI04WaJVvtocST","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_48e5a06d3fb2469ba8e648e4","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-erlang-toolchain-empirical-research/plans/opencode-rollout.md","offset":110,"limit":230},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-erlang-toolchain-empirical-research/plans/opencode-rollout.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n110: Lesson: a board note written under time pressure to prevent a duplicate dispatch\n111: should describe the *risk*, not assert unverified internal state (\"worker is live\")\n112: as fact -- the next tick then has to re-derive ground truth from disk anyway. Cheaper\n113: to just mark `status: blocked-pending-verification` and let the next tick check\n114: `git log`/`pgrep` itself.\n115: \n116: ## Incident: erlang-target-research (gh:163) gave up on a toolchain-check denial (2026-09-01)\n117: \n118: 18 steps of legitimate research (read `docs/reference/types/{stream,str,char}.md`,\n119: `research-log/index.md`, `ESCALATION.md`, grepped `Backend::` in `src/lib.rs`, read\n120: backend emitters) then tried `which erl escript erlc; erl -eval '...halt().' -noshell`\n121: to check whether an Erlang toolchain exists on the host -- denied by the permission\n122: classifier -- and exited immediately after, zero commits, no `plans/*.md` written, no\n123: ESCALATION.md for this run. Landed on a lane whose branch tip happened to equal the\n124: `to-merge-1788204752` accumulator (its dispatch base, since that accumulator was the\n125: largest live one at dispatch time) -- misread by the gate script's `ahead=7 dirty=0`\n126: signal as \"worker exited, landable,\" when actually zero commits were this worker's own.\n127: \n128: Root cause: the task never needed to actually run Erlang. The brief asks for a design\n129: survey (process/effect model, pattern matching, immutability) against docs already in\n130: the repo and toylang's own backend source -- a desk review, not an empirical one. The\n131: worker chose to verify toolchain presence anyway, hit the sandbox wall, and gave up\n132: rather than continuing with the read-only research it had already started.\n133: \n134: Fix: rebriefed with an explicit \"no toolchain/execution needed, docs+source read only,\n135: write findings to plans/erlang-target-research.md\" instruction. | $0.01, 18 steps,\n136: zero commits, one coordinator rebrief | No -- the `which`/`erl -eval` denial is sandbox\n137: policy (arbitrary binary execution), not opencode-specific; the give-up-after-one-denial\n138: behavior is the same shape as issue-108/125 above.\n139: \n140: ## Ruling: escalation/decision tracking moves off GitHub issues (2026-09-01)\n141: \n142: Maintainer answer to the coordinator-escalation-brief-phrasing-experiment round\n143: (inbox record, 2026-09-01T20:36:51Z): \"we can just have a local inbox for such\n144: cases, no need to rely on github issues; i guess actually we can migrate the\n145: whole flow to work entirely on files committed into the repo at this stage.\"\n146: \n147: Immediate action taken: the blocked note (brief-phrasing-experiment) got a board\n148: row with no `issue:` field instead of a `gh issue create` retry -- board.yaml\n149: already supports issue-less rows, so this needed no new mechanism.\n150: \n151: Open, not yet scoped: the maintainer's broader remark reads as a ruling that the\n152: whole escalation/decision-tracking flow (currently: compose note -> file gh\n153: issue -> board row references it) should move to files committed in the repo\n154: instead of GitHub issues. This is a process/tooling change bigger than one note\n155: and hasn't been designed yet -- needs a decide row (what replaces \"issue:\n156: gh:N\" as the cross-reference key; how history/discussion attach to a row\n157: without an issue thread) before any dispatch. Not board-added yet; flagging\n158: here so the next tick that touches escalation composition sees it before\n159: defaulting back to gh issue create.\n160: \n161: ## Incident: issue-154 stuck at 3 commitless runs on a self-inflicted denial loop (2026-09-01)\n162: \n163: Root cause (from `20260901-221717-issue-154.jsonl`): the worker built real work\n164: (input-type-annotation + tail-pipe |>, 173 insertions across 4 files, own\n165: ESCALATION.md justifying the scope), then verified it by writing\n166: `scratch_tailpipe.toy` and repeatedly running `cargo run -q -- run\n167: scratch_tailpipe.toy` -- direct binary execution, always denied. The run ended\n168: mid-loop on that exact denial (`UnknownError: The user rejected permission to\n169: use this specific tool call`), with nothing ever committed. Same failure shape\n170: as issue-93 and issue-155's \"permission denial on direct binary execution\".\n171: \n172: Fix applied: added `cargo run -- run <file>` / direct binary execution to the\n173: KNOWN DENIALS boilerplate in `dispatch-worker.sh`, pointing workers at\n174: `tests/corpus/*.yaml` + `just check` (AGENTS.md's sanctioned verification path)\n175: instead of scratch-file-plus-manual-run. Rebriefed issue-154 to keep its\n176: existing uncommitted work, convert the scratch file to a corpus case, and\n177: commit once `just check` is green.\n178: \n179: ## Incident: issue-149 stuck at 2 commitless runs, all-or-nothing scope on a 6-backend task (2026-09-02)\n180: \n181: Root cause (from `20260830-220941-issue-149.jsonl` and `20260901-232217-issue-149.jsonl`):\n182: both runs treated \"Implement Float across the backends\" (gh:149, board row\n183: float-build) as a research task and spent their entire step budget writing and\n184: running cross-language probe scripts (Go, Lua, C via `cc`, jq, Node, Python) to\n185: characterize each backend's native double-to-string formatting, then hit the\n186: step limit with zero commits both times. This is legitimate groundwork (per\n187: plans/questions.md#q37, printing format is explicitly unruled per-backend\n188: conformance work) but the runs never got past survey mode into writing the\n189: actual formatter code, and held all 6 backends' work uncommitted while\n190: chasing full completion instead of landing backends incrementally.\n191: \n192: Diagnosed directly rather than dispatching an investigation worker (the\n193: evidence was conclusive from the logs alone). Rebriefed issue-149 in place:\n194: pointed at the existing probe output instead of re-deriving it, named ADR\n195: 0007 + JS's Number.prototype.toString() as the reference algorithm, and\n196: required committing one backend's formatter at a time rather than holding\n197: everything uncommitted until all six are done.\n198: \n199: ## issue-158 stuck lane: already explained, no dispatch needed (2026-09-02)\n200: \n201: `stuck-issue-158-investigation` asked to diagnose why issue-158 (shell-out-build,\n202: gh:158) had two commitless runs. Already answered on the board itself:\n203: `shell-out-build` carries `needs: [stdout-stderr-effect-model-design]`, and that\n204: decide row already has a composed, pending grill round\n205: (docs/.grill/stdout-stderr-effect-model.round.yaml) asking exactly the Q35\n206: question both runs got stuck on. Archived the investigation row instead of\n207: dispatching a worker to re-derive an answer that's already on file -- the real\n208: next step is the maintainer answering the pending round, not more automated\n209: investigation.\n210: \n211: ## Incident: issue-168 investigation dispatch used the wrong brief wrapper (2026-09-02)\n212: \n213: Dispatched `stuck-issue-168-investigation` via `dispatch-worker.sh 168 \"<investigation\n214: brief>\"` without `BRIEF_RAW=1`. The script's default wrapper always prepends \"Your\n215: task is GitHub issue #$N: run `gh issue view $N` ... $BRIEF\" -- for issue-168 that's\n216: the *original* feature issue, not \"investigate why this lane is stuck.\" The live log\n217: confirms the worker read gh:168's real issue text and started exploring the actual\n218: offload/vectorizability feature (reading tir.rs, draft.md's offload section, corpus\n219: tests) instead of the frozen incident evidence at\n220: plans/incidents/issue-168-20260902/. Attempted to kill the misdirected process and\n221: was blocked by the auto-mode classifier (process-kill outside the sanctioned\n222: scripts) -- per house rule, not retrying through another channel. Letting this run\n223: finish; it will likely land as another commitless run since the worker is doing\n224: the wrong task. Next tick should redispatch issue-168's investigation with\n225: `BRIEF_RAW=1` (the script's own doc comment already names this as the case\n226: \"research dispatches, custom continuations\" need), and this note stands as a\n227: reminder for every future stuck-lane investigation dispatch: always set\n228: `BRIEF_RAW=1`, never let the standard build-brief wrapper attach to an\n229: investigation task.\n230: \n231: ## Incident: land-lane.sh's auto-commit only covers tracked files -- untracked\n232: ## research findings are at risk of being cleaned away (2026-09-02)\n233: \n234: Found four orphaned research lanes (issue-trait-interface-research,\n235: issue-signature-matching-deeper-research, issue-http-query-sugar-research,\n236: issue-recursive-descent-order-research) with no activity since 2026-09-01\n237: ~21:30, invisible to the current stuck-lane snapshot (their names don't match\n238: the numeric `issue-<N>` pattern the tracker expects). Two of them\n239: (trait-interface-research, signature-matching-deeper-research) have real\n240: findings files sitting **untracked** (`?? plans/<name>.md`) -- never `git\n241: add`ed, so they are not \"tracked dirty\" and the 2026-09-02 auto-commit ruling\n242: in land-lane.sh (`git -C \"$d\" add -u` then commit) does not pick them up.\n243: Worse: the very next step in `land land-lane.sh` runs `git clean -fdq` on any\n244: remaining untracked files as \"sanctioned scratch\" -- if this were run against\n245: these lanes as-is, it would silently **delete** the findings before they were\n246: ever persisted. Did not run `land-lane.sh land` on these two lanes to avoid\n247: that. Dispatched continuation workers instead (BRIEF_RAW=1) whose only job is\n248: `git add` + `git commit` for the existing findings file, so the tracked-dirty\n249: path lands safely on the next `land-lane.sh` pass. The other two lanes\n250: (http-query-sugar-research, recursive-descent-order-research) have zero diff\n251: from main -- they produced nothing and need re-investigation, not landing.\n252: Follow-up: land-lane.sh's auto-commit should `git add -A` (not `-u`) when the\n253: gate is green, or the cleanup step should skip files that look like research\n254: output (`plans/*.md`) -- filed as a board row.\n255: \n256: \n257: ## Investigation: stuck lane issue-170 (gh:170, erlang-toolchain-empirical-research) (2026-09-01\n258: \n259: Lane stats at capture: 1 run, 0 commits, clean tree, ahead  ​0, dead 22h. The\n260: event-log tail ends after a completed tool call with no final message, no commit,\n261: no write -- consistent with the step-budget exhaustion already seen on issue-116/133,\n262: though not provable from the tail alone. The branch tip is `d9896ec` (the module-routing\n263: spike commit), so no lane work ever happened on top of the dispatch base.\n264: \n265: What the single run actually did (from the jsonl tail): read the issue body via\n266: `gh issue view 170`; failed to find `plans/erlang-target-research.md` in the worktree\n267: (it is absent from this lane base -- it landed on main via the to-merge accumulator\n268: only after the base was cut),and recovered it via `git show 9b9cec3:...` from git\n269: history;read the desk research;ran `which erl erlc escript erl_call` -- ALLOWED by the\n270: permission gate, found nothing on PATH;(in run 1, the compound `which ...; erl\n271: -eval ...` was denied, so the classifier's edge is the compound form, not `which`\n272: itself)ran `erl -noshell -eval ...` -- DENIED (\"The user rejected permission to use\n273: this specific tool call\");verified `/usr/bin`, `/usr/local/bin`, `/opt`, `/snap` and\n274: `/usr/lib/erlang` -- all empty;and stopped, uncommitted, with the last reasoning\n275: still mid-plan (\"let me confirm by attempting to run erl directly\").\n276: \n277: Diagnosis: this was primarily a **brief-clarity** failure, compounded by task shape\n278: and an asymmetric permission allow-list;,not a capability gap:\n279: \n280: - The brief's decision tree had two branches: denial -> document-and-stop; not-denied\n281:   + available -> verify empirically. The observed case -- not denied (the `which`\n282:   succeeded) but also not available -- had no branch. The worker's own reasoning\n283:   identified \"a third case: allowed but not present\" and then had no scripted\n284:   done-state to land on.\n285: - The brief described run 1's denial as \"`which erl` / `erl -eval`\" was denied,;\n286:   collapsing a compound-command denial into two individual denials. When `which`\n287:   alone succeeded, the worker concluded the denial case was off the table and pursued\n288:   running `erl` anyway -- exactly the outcome the denial branch existed to prevent. The\n289:   actual classifier edges: compound shell forms auto-reject, executing a non-whitelisted\n290:   binary is denied, `which` alone is fine.\n291: - The brief referenced \"plans/opencode-rollout.md, incident 2026-09-01\" -- but this\n292:   lane's copy of that file has no such entry (the branch predates the incident-log\n293:   commit),and the addendum target `plans/erlang-target-research.md` is absent from the\n294:   lane base. The worker recovered both from git history, at the cost of several steps\n295:   and early confusion.\n296: \n297: - Task shape: the core ask -- \"actually install/verify the Erlang toolchain and run the\n298:   documented-semantics comparison against real erl/erlc output\" -- is impossible on\n299:   this host: no Erlang binaries exist (PATH + five filesystem locations verified empty),running\n300:   `erl` is denied by the permission allow-list;,and installation (apt/snap/network or\n301:   executing an installer) would almost certainly hit the same walls (unverified;not\n302:   attempted). The only deliverable available is the negative finding, which the brief\n303:   half-defined.\n304: - The permission system is an aggravator, not the root: its per-command behavior is\n305:   stable but undiscussed at the granularity the brief needed, and the deny message \"The user\n306:   rejected permission\" doesn't name the deny system, so a worker cannot tell policy from\n307:   a human intervention.\n308: - Not a capability gap: the worker read the missing file from git history, named the\n309:   unscripted case explicitly, adapted after the `erl` denial (via `ls` checks, not a second\n310:   execution channel),and ran far past where run 1 gave up. Nothing in its reasoning\n311:   suggests it couldn't have written the addendum;the failure is that no instruction told\n312:   it the absence was itself the deliverable, plus a likely step-budget exhaustion.\n313: \n314: Recommendation:\n315: \n316: - **Reshape gh:170's row** from \"empirically verify Erlang\" to \"determine whether an\n317:   Erlang toolchain can be exercised here, and record the finding\". The brief must enumerate\n318:   the full outcome space: (a) toolchain found -> run the comparison script against real\n319:   erl, append results;(b) toolchain absent -> append an addendum to\n320:   plans/erlang-target-research.md documenting: no binaries on PATH or in /usr/bin,,\n321:   /usr/local/bin, /opt, /snap, /usr/lib/erlang;executing erl is additionally denied\n322:   by the allow-list (observed: \"The user rejected permission\");installation was not\n323:   attempted and is presumed blocked the same way (flag as unverified;;and stop -- that\n324:   addendum is the deliverable, not a failure.(c) any check denied -> document exactly what\n325:   was denied and stop.\n326: - **Then close gh:170**:the empirical comparison cannot happen here, the desk research\n327:   (plans/erlang-target-research.md) remains the deliverable for gh:163,and surface the\n328:   maintainer's flagged concurrency open item as its own board row now -- the issue body's\n329:   \"once this lands\" condition has effectively become \"cannot land here\"..\n330: - **Dispatch-template fixes**, generalizable: (1) any \"verify tool X\" brief must enumerate\n331:   all three outcomes (present / absent / denied)and define a done-state for each -- \"a tool\n332:   that does not exist is a complete finding, not a stop condition\", the mirror of the existing\n333:   \"a rejected tool call is not a stop condition\" rule;(2) describe the permission system's\n334:   edges as command classes, not as the compound commands that triggered them;(3) when a\n335:   brief references a file that may not exist on the lane base, either cut the lane from a base\n336:   that contains it or give the worker a `git show` pointer;(4) the step-budget exhaustion\n337:   has now hit three lanes with zero commits each (issue-116, issue-133, this one)--\n338:   worth instructing workers to write and commit partial findings before further verification,\n339:   or raising the budget;(5) for the 30-lane review: this lane did NOT reproduce run 1's\n\n(Showing lines 110-339 of 1099. Use offset=340 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"Lesson: a board note written under time pressure to prevent a duplicate dispatch\nshould describe the *risk*, not assert unverified internal state (\"worker is live\")\nas fact -- the next tick then has to re-derive ground truth from disk anyway. Cheaper\nto just mark `status: blocked-pending-verification` and let the next tick check\n`git log`/`pgrep` itself.\n\n## Incident: erlang-target-research (gh:163) gave up on a toolchain-check denial (2026-09-01)\n\n18 steps of legitimate research (read `docs/reference/types/{stream,str,char}.md`,\n`research-log/index.md`, `ESCALATION.md`, grepped `Backend::` in `src/lib.rs`, read\nbackend emitters) then tried `which erl escript erlc; erl -eval '...halt().' -noshell`\nto check whether an Erlang toolchain exists on the host -- denied by the permission\nclassifier -- and exited immediately after, zero commits, no `plans/*.md` written, no\nESCALATION.md for this run. Landed on a lane whose branch tip happened to equal the\n`to-merge-1788204752` accumulator (its dispatch base, since that accumulator was the\nlargest live one at dispatch time) -- misread by the gate script's `ahead=7 dirty=0`\nsignal as \"worker exited, landable,\" when actually zero commits were this worker's own.\n\nRoot cause: the task never needed to actually run Erlang. The brief asks for a design\nsurvey (process/effect model, pattern matching, immutability) against docs already in","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-erlang-toolchain-empirical-research/plans/opencode-rollout.md","text":"Lesson: a board note written under time pressure to prevent a duplicate dispatch\nshould describe the *risk*, not assert unverified internal state (\"worker is live\")\nas fact -- the next tick then has to re-derive ground truth from disk anyway. Cheaper\nto just mark `status: blocked-pending-verification` and let the next tick check\n`git log`/`pgrep` itself.\n\n## Incident: erlang-target-research (gh:163) gave up on a toolchain-check denial (2026-09-01)\n\n18 steps of legitimate research (read `docs/reference/types/{stream,str,char}.md`,\n`research-log/index.md`, `ESCALATION.md`, grepped `Backend::` in `src/lib.rs`, read\nbackend emitters) then tried `which erl escript erlc; erl -eval '...halt().' -noshell`\nto check whether an Erlang toolchain exists on the host -- denied by the permission\nclassifier -- and exited immediately after, zero commits, no `plans/*.md` written, no\nESCALATION.md for this run. Landed on a lane whose branch tip happened to equal the\n`to-merge-1788204752` accumulator (its dispatch base, since that accumulator was the\nlargest live one at dispatch time) -- misread by the gate script's `ahead=7 dirty=0`\nsignal as \"worker exited, landable,\" when actually zero commits were this worker's own.\n\nRoot cause: the task never needed to actually run Erlang. The brief asks for a design\nsurvey (process/effect model, pattern matching, immutability) against docs already in\nthe repo and toylang's own backend source -- a desk review, not an empirical one. The\nworker chose to verify toolchain presence anyway, hit the sandbox wall, and gave up\nrather than continuing with the read-only research it had already started.\n\nFix: rebriefed with an explicit \"no toolchain/execution needed, docs+source read only,\nwrite findings to plans/erlang-target-research.md\" instruction. | $0.01, 18 steps,\nzero commits, one coordinator rebrief | No -- the `which`/`erl -eval` denial is sandbox\npolicy (arbitrary binary execution), not opencode-specific; the give-up-after-one-denial\nbehavior is the same shape as issue-108/125 above.\n\n## Ruling: escalation/decision tracking moves off GitHub issues (2026-09-01)\n\nMaintainer answer to the coordinator-escalation-brief-phrasing-experiment round\n(inbox record, 2026-09-01T20:36:51Z): \"we can just have a local inbox for such\ncases, no need to rely on github issues; i guess actually we can migrate the\nwhole flow to work entirely on files committed into the repo at this stage.\"\n\nImmediate action taken: the blocked note (brief-phrasing-experiment) got a board\nrow with no `issue:` field instead of a `gh issue create` retry -- board.yaml\nalready supports issue-less rows, so this needed no new mechanism.\n\nOpen, not yet scoped: the maintainer's broader remark reads as a ruling that the\nwhole escalation/decision-tracking flow (currently: compose note -> file gh\nissue -> board row references it) should move to files committed in the repo\ninstead of GitHub issues. This is a process/tooling change bigger than one note\nand hasn't been designed yet -- needs a decide row (what replaces \"issue:\ngh:N\" as the cross-reference key; how history/discussion attach to a row\nwithout an issue thread) before any dispatch. Not board-added yet; flagging\nhere so the next tick that touches escalation composition sees it before\ndefaulting back to gh issue create.\n\n## Incident: issue-154 stuck at 3 commitless runs on a self-inflicted denial loop (2026-09-01)\n\nRoot cause (from `20260901-221717-issue-154.jsonl`): the worker built real work\n(input-type-annotation + tail-pipe |>, 173 insertions across 4 files, own\nESCALATION.md justifying the scope), then verified it by writing\n`scratch_tailpipe.toy` and repeatedly running `cargo run -q -- run\nscratch_tailpipe.toy` -- direct binary execution, always denied. The run ended\nmid-loop on that exact denial (`UnknownError: The user rejected permission to\nuse this specific tool call`), with nothing ever committed. Same failure shape\nas issue-93 and issue-155's \"permission denial on direct binary execution\".\n\nFix applied: added `cargo run -- run <file>` / direct binary execution to the\nKNOWN DENIALS boilerplate in `dispatch-worker.sh`, pointing workers at\n`tests/corpus/*.yaml` + `just check` (AGENTS.md's sanctioned verification path)\ninstead of scratch-file-plus-manual-run. Rebriefed issue-154 to keep its\nexisting uncommitted work, convert the scratch file to a corpus case, and\ncommit once `just check` is green.\n\n## Incident: issue-149 stuck at 2 commitless runs, all-or-nothing scope on a 6-backend task (2026-09-02)\n\nRoot cause (from `20260830-220941-issue-149.jsonl` and `20260901-232217-issue-149.jsonl`):\nboth runs treated \"Implement Float across the backends\" (gh:149, board row\nfloat-build) as a research task and spent their entire step budget writing and\nrunning cross-language probe scripts (Go, Lua, C via `cc`, jq, Node, Python) to\ncharacterize each backend's native double-to-string formatting, then hit the\nstep limit with zero commits both times. This is legitimate groundwork (per\nplans/questions.md#q37, printing format is explicitly unruled per-backend\nconformance work) but the runs never got past survey mode into writing the\nactual formatter code, and held all 6 backends' work uncommitted while\nchasing full completion instead of landing backends incrementally.\n\nDiagnosed directly rather than dispatching an investigation worker (the\nevidence was conclusive from the logs alone). Rebriefed issue-149 in place:\npointed at the existing probe output instead of re-deriving it, named ADR\n0007 + JS's Number.prototype.toString() as the reference algorithm, and\nrequired committing one backend's formatter at a time rather than holding\neverything uncommitted until all six are done.\n\n## issue-158 stuck lane: already explained, no dispatch needed (2026-09-02)\n\n`stuck-issue-158-investigation` asked to diagnose why issue-158 (shell-out-build,\ngh:158) had two commitless runs. Already answered on the board itself:\n`shell-out-build` carries `needs: [stdout-stderr-effect-model-design]`, and that\ndecide row already has a composed, pending grill round\n(docs/.grill/stdout-stderr-effect-model.round.yaml) asking exactly the Q35\nquestion both runs got stuck on. Archived the investigation row instead of\ndispatching a worker to re-derive an answer that's already on file -- the real\nnext step is the maintainer answering the pending round, not more automated\ninvestigation.\n\n## Incident: issue-168 investigation dispatch used the wrong brief wrapper (2026-09-02)\n\nDispatched `stuck-issue-168-investigation` via `dispatch-worker.sh 168 \"<investigation\nbrief>\"` without `BRIEF_RAW=1`. The script's default wrapper always prepends \"Your\ntask is GitHub issue #$N: run `gh issue view $N` ... $BRIEF\" -- for issue-168 that's\nthe *original* feature issue, not \"investigate why this lane is stuck.\" The live log\nconfirms the worker read gh:168's real issue text and started exploring the actual\noffload/vectorizability feature (reading tir.rs, draft.md's offload section, corpus\ntests) instead of the frozen incident evidence at\nplans/incidents/issue-168-20260902/. Attempted to kill the misdirected process and\nwas blocked by the auto-mode classifier (process-kill outside the sanctioned\nscripts) -- per house rule, not retrying through another channel. Letting this run\nfinish; it will likely land as another commitless run since the worker is doing\nthe wrong task. Next tick should redispatch issue-168's investigation with\n`BRIEF_RAW=1` (the script's own doc comment already names this as the case\n\"research dispatches, custom continuations\" need), and this note stands as a\nreminder for every future stuck-lane investigation dispatch: always set\n`BRIEF_RAW=1`, never let the standard build-brief wrapper attach to an\ninvestigation task.\n\n## Incident: land-lane.sh's auto-commit only covers tracked files -- untracked\n## research findings are at risk of being cleaned away (2026-09-02)\n\nFound four orphaned research lanes (issue-trait-interface-research,\nissue-signature-matching-deeper-research, issue-http-query-sugar-research,\nissue-recursive-descent-order-research) with no activity since 2026-09-01\n~21:30, invisible to the current stuck-lane snapshot (their names don't match\nthe numeric `issue-<N>` pattern the tracker expects). Two of them\n(trait-interface-research, signature-matching-deeper-research) have real\nfindings files sitting **untracked** (`?? plans/<name>.md`) -- never `git\nadd`ed, so they are not \"tracked dirty\" and the 2026-09-02 auto-commit ruling\nin land-lane.sh (`git -C \"$d\" add -u` then commit) does not pick them up.\nWorse: the very next step in `land land-lane.sh` runs `git clean -fdq` on any\nremaining untracked files as \"sanctioned scratch\" -- if this were run against\nthese lanes as-is, it would silently **delete** the findings before they were\never persisted. Did not run `land-lane.sh land` on these two lanes to avoid\nthat. Dispatched continuation workers instead (BRIEF_RAW=1) whose only job is\n`git add` + `git commit` for the existing findings file, so the tracked-dirty\npath lands safely on the next `land-lane.sh` pass. The other two lanes\n(http-query-sugar-research, recursive-descent-order-research) have zero diff\nfrom main -- they produced nothing and need re-investigation, not landing.\nFollow-up: land-lane.sh's auto-commit should `git add -A` (not `-u`) when the\ngate is green, or the cleanup step should skip files that look like research\noutput (`plans/*.md`) -- filed as a board row.\n\n\n## Investigation: stuck lane issue-170 (gh:170, erlang-toolchain-empirical-research) (2026-09-01\n\nLane stats at capture: 1 run, 0 commits, clean tree, ahead  ​0, dead 22h. The\nevent-log tail ends after a completed tool call with no final message, no commit,\nno write -- consistent with the step-budget exhaustion already seen on issue-116/133,\nthough not provable from the tail alone. The branch tip is `d9896ec` (the module-routing\nspike commit), so no lane work ever happened on top of the dispatch base.\n\nWhat the single run actually did (from the jsonl tail): read the issue body via\n`gh issue view 170`; failed to find `plans/erlang-target-research.md` in the worktree\n(it is absent from this lane base -- it landed on main via the to-merge accumulator\nonly after the base was cut),and recovered it via `git show 9b9cec3:...` from git\nhistory;read the desk research;ran `which erl erlc escript erl_call` -- ALLOWED by the\npermission gate, found nothing on PATH;(in run 1, the compound `which ...; erl\n-eval ...` was denied, so the classifier's edge is the compound form, not `which`\nitself)ran `erl -noshell -eval ...` -- DENIED (\"The user rejected permission to use\nthis specific tool call\");verified `/usr/bin`, `/usr/local/bin`, `/opt`, `/snap` and\n`/usr/lib/erlang` -- all empty;and stopped, uncommitted, with the last reasoning\nstill mid-plan (\"let me confirm by attempting to run erl directly\").\n\nDiagnosis: this was primarily a **brief-clarity** failure, compounded by task shape\nand an asymmetric permission allow-list;,not a capability gap:\n\n- The brief's decision tree had two branches: denial -> document-and-stop; not-denied\n  + available -> verify empirically. The observed case -- not denied (the `which`\n  succeeded) but also not available -- had no branch. The worker's own reasoning\n  identified \"a third case: allowed but not present\" and then had no scripted\n  done-state to land on.\n- The brief described run 1's denial as \"`which erl` / `erl -eval`\" was denied,;\n  collapsing a compound-command denial into two individual denials. When `which`\n  alone succeeded, the worker concluded the denial case was off the table and pursued\n  running `erl` anyway -- exactly the outcome the denial branch existed to prevent. The\n  actual classifier edges: compound shell forms auto-reject, executing a non-whitelisted\n  binary is denied, `which` alone is fine.\n- The brief referenced \"plans/opencode-rollout.md, incident 2026-09-01\" -- but this\n  lane's copy of that file has no such entry (the branch predates the incident-log\n  commit),and the addendum target `plans/erlang-target-research.md` is absent from the\n  lane base. The worker recovered both from git history, at the cost of several steps\n  and early confusion.\n\n- Task shape: the core ask -- \"actually install/verify the Erlang toolchain and run the\n  documented-semantics comparison against real erl/erlc output\" -- is impossible on\n  this host: no Erlang binaries exist (PATH + five filesystem locations verified empty),running\n  `erl` is denied by the permission allow-list;,and installation (apt/snap/network or\n  executing an installer) would almost certainly hit the same walls (unverified;not\n  attempted). The only deliverable available is the negative finding, which the brief\n  half-defined.\n- The permission system is an aggravator, not the root: its per-command behavior is\n  stable but undiscussed at the granularity the brief needed, and the deny message \"The user\n  rejected permission\" doesn't name the deny system, so a worker cannot tell policy from\n  a human intervention.\n- Not a capability gap: the worker read the missing file from git history, named the\n  unscripted case explicitly, adapted after the `erl` denial (via `ls` checks, not a second\n  execution channel),and ran far past where run 1 gave up. Nothing in its reasoning\n  suggests it couldn't have written the addendum;the failure is that no instruction told\n  it the absence was itself the deliverable, plus a likely step-budget exhaustion.\n\nRecommendation:\n\n- **Reshape gh:170's row** from \"empirically verify Erlang\" to \"determine whether an\n  Erlang toolchain can be exercised here, and record the finding\". The brief must enumerate\n  the full outcome space: (a) toolchain found -> run the comparison script against real\n  erl, append results;(b) toolchain absent -> append an addendum to\n  plans/erlang-target-research.md documenting: no binaries on PATH or in /usr/bin,,\n  /usr/local/bin, /opt, /snap, /usr/lib/erlang;executing erl is additionally denied\n  by the allow-list (observed: \"The user rejected permission\");installation was not\n  attempted and is presumed blocked the same way (flag as unverified;;and stop -- that\n  addendum is the deliverable, not a failure.(c) any check denied -> document exactly what\n  was denied and stop.\n- **Then close gh:170**:the empirical comparison cannot happen here, the desk research\n  (plans/erlang-target-research.md) remains the deliverable for gh:163,and surface the\n  maintainer's flagged concurrency open item as its own board row now -- the issue body's\n  \"once this lands\" condition has effectively become \"cannot land here\"..\n- **Dispatch-template fixes**, generalizable: (1) any \"verify tool X\" brief must enumerate\n  all three outcomes (present / absent / denied)and define a done-state for each -- \"a tool\n  that does not exist is a complete finding, not a stop condition\", the mirror of the existing\n  \"a rejected tool call is not a stop condition\" rule;(2) describe the permission system's\n  edges as command classes, not as the compound commands that triggered them;(3) when a\n  brief references a file that may not exist on the lane base, either cut the lane from a base\n  that contains it or give the worker a `git show` pointer;(4) the step-budget exhaustion\n  has now hit three lanes with zero commits each (issue-116, issue-133, this one)--\n  worth instructing workers to write and commit partial findings before further verification,\n  or raising the budget;(5) for the 30-lane review: this lane did NOT reproduce run 1's","lineStart":110,"lineEnd":339,"totalLines":1099,"truncated":true}},"title":"plans/opencode-rollout.md","time":{"start":1788705258935,"end":1788705258952}},"id":"prt_077243db50014NCmyObrOywQu5","sessionID":"ses_f88dc29b9ffeZI04WaJVvtocST","messageID":"msg_077241f6e001h55pBKir28HncB"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788705258970,"sessionID":"ses_f88dc29b9ffeZI04WaJVvtocST","part":{"id":"prt_077243dd7001MjHuh50naNFPiA","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"1d59abbc7f08872ac99e40bf7e75dfb5d0e17f8d","messageID":"msg_077241f6e001h55pBKir28HncB","sessionID":"ses_f88dc29b9ffeZI04WaJVvtocST","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":28574,"input":3437,"output":145,"reasoning":928,"cache":{"write":0,"read":24064}},"cost":0.000519582084}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788705260060,"sessionID":"ses_f88dc29b9ffeZI04WaJVvtocST","part":{"id":"prt_07724421500155EBKEp4bvXOff","messageID":"msg_077243de5001T2OnvL13h4x440","sessionID":"ses_f88dc29b9ffeZI04WaJVvtocST","snapshot":"1d59abbc7f08872ac99e40bf7e75dfb5d0e17f8d","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788705288744,"sessionID":"ses_f88dc29b9ffeZI04WaJVvtocST","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_19c4307e95e9462890bbf6ae","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-erlang-toolchain-empirical-research/plans/opencode-rollout.md","limit":40,"offset":339},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-erlang-toolchain-empirical-research/plans/opencode-rollout.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n339:   or raising the budget;(5) for the 30-lane review: this lane did NOT reproduce run 1's\n340:   give-up-on-first-denial shape -- the worker pushed through multiple denials and checks -- but\n341:   landed in the same commitless zero-output state, because it ran out of scripted outcomes\n342: \n343:   before it ran out of steps. The bottleneck is the decision tree, not tenacity.\n344: \n345: ## Investigation: stuck lane issue-float-build-python (2026-09-04)\n346: \n347: Verdict: **tooling/permission trap**, compounded by a brief-wording gap\n348: and a content-channel corruption, not a capability gap. The lane's one run\n349: produced real, committable work -- the entire Python float emitter diff\n350: (src/emit_py.rs, +71/-3, ASCII-clean) -- but died at a denied scratch-probe\n351: execution with nothing committed, no tests written, no `just check` run, no\n352: ESCALATION.md. Lane stats at capture: 1 run,  0 commits, tracked-dirty\n353: tree, dead ~38h (evidence fetched from git via ca1f011, since the lane checkout\n354: predates the freeze note).\n355: \n356: What the run did: read the JS reference tests, implemented FLOAT_HELPER\n357: (Python repr reshaped into JS's String(number) spelling: exponent expansion,\n358: `.0` strip, exponent unpadding),, DIVF_HELPER (IEEE zero-divisor answers through\n359: tl_divf),,, wired show()/expr()/arith() and the helper gating,, then cleaned the file\n360: to plain ASCII. It then planned backend_py.rs tests (mirroring the JS five plus\n361: exponent-boundary cases),, got worried whether Rust's `f64::to_string()`\n362: (`src/float.rs::lit` is `n.to_string()`) might emit a plain-decimal literal Python\n363: would parse as an int (a real hazard: tl_float's reshaping only fires when repr\n364: has an `e` or `.0`), wrote f64_probe.rs, compiled it with `rustc` (allowed),\n365: tried `./f64_probe` -- denied (direct binary execution), and ended right there.\n366: \n367: \n368: \n369: Two aggravators made the run as expensive as it was. First, the worker's own\n370: tool-call text got corrupted in transit, intermittently: combining accents inserted before digits, `)` -> `[]`, `,` -> `[],`, `the`/`ther` swaps,\n371: dropped parens -- across write/edit/bash-c strings. It diagnosed this forensically\n372: (hexdumps, sub-string probes,)and adapted (single-line edits,, \"no risky\n373: characters\", a `python3` script for the comment surgery the edit tool couldn't\n374: match), but the forensics consumed roughly the majority of the run's steps. This\n375: is an environment problem, not a capability failure -- and worth a landing-time\n376: scan: this lane's diff is clean only because the worker ran its own byte scan;a\n377: lane that skipped that would have committed the corruption silently. Second, the\n378: fatal probe question (Rust's f64 Display spelling at exponent boundaries)has no\n\n(Showing lines 339-378 of 1099. Use offset=379 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"  or raising the budget;(5) for the 30-lane review: this lane did NOT reproduce run 1's\n  give-up-on-first-denial shape -- the worker pushed through multiple denials and checks -- but\n  landed in the same commitless zero-output state, because it ran out of scripted outcomes\n\n  before it ran out of steps. The bottleneck is the decision tree, not tenacity.\n\n## Investigation: stuck lane issue-float-build-python (2026-09-04)\n\nVerdict: **tooling/permission trap**, compounded by a brief-wording gap\nand a content-channel corruption, not a capability gap. The lane's one run\nproduced real, committable work -- the entire Python float emitter diff\n(src/emit_py.rs, +71/-3, ASCII-clean) -- but died at a denied scratch-probe\nexecution with nothing committed, no tests written, no `just check` run, no\nESCALATION.md. Lane stats at capture: 1 run,  0 commits, tracked-dirty\ntree, dead ~38h (evidence fetched from git via ca1f011, since the lane checkout\npredates the freeze note).\n\nWhat the run did: read the JS reference tests, implemented FLOAT_HELPER\n(Python repr reshaped into JS's String(number) spelling: exponent expansion,\n`.0` strip, exponent unpadding),, DIVF_HELPER (IEEE zero-divisor answers through","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-erlang-toolchain-empirical-research/plans/opencode-rollout.md","text":"  or raising the budget;(5) for the 30-lane review: this lane did NOT reproduce run 1's\n  give-up-on-first-denial shape -- the worker pushed through multiple denials and checks -- but\n  landed in the same commitless zero-output state, because it ran out of scripted outcomes\n\n  before it ran out of steps. The bottleneck is the decision tree, not tenacity.\n\n## Investigation: stuck lane issue-float-build-python (2026-09-04)\n\nVerdict: **tooling/permission trap**, compounded by a brief-wording gap\nand a content-channel corruption, not a capability gap. The lane's one run\nproduced real, committable work -- the entire Python float emitter diff\n(src/emit_py.rs, +71/-3, ASCII-clean) -- but died at a denied scratch-probe\nexecution with nothing committed, no tests written, no `just check` run, no\nESCALATION.md. Lane stats at capture: 1 run,  0 commits, tracked-dirty\ntree, dead ~38h (evidence fetched from git via ca1f011, since the lane checkout\npredates the freeze note).\n\nWhat the run did: read the JS reference tests, implemented FLOAT_HELPER\n(Python repr reshaped into JS's String(number) spelling: exponent expansion,\n`.0` strip, exponent unpadding),, DIVF_HELPER (IEEE zero-divisor answers through\ntl_divf),,, wired show()/expr()/arith() and the helper gating,, then cleaned the file\nto plain ASCII. It then planned backend_py.rs tests (mirroring the JS five plus\nexponent-boundary cases),, got worried whether Rust's `f64::to_string()`\n(`src/float.rs::lit` is `n.to_string()`) might emit a plain-decimal literal Python\nwould parse as an int (a real hazard: tl_float's reshaping only fires when repr\nhas an `e` or `.0`), wrote f64_probe.rs, compiled it with `rustc` (allowed),\ntried `./f64_probe` -- denied (direct binary execution), and ended right there.\n\n\n\nTwo aggravators made the run as expensive as it was. First, the worker's own\ntool-call text got corrupted in transit, intermittently: combining accents inserted before digits, `)` -> `[]`, `,` -> `[],`, `the`/`ther` swaps,\ndropped parens -- across write/edit/bash-c strings. It diagnosed this forensically\n(hexdumps, sub-string probes,)and adapted (single-line edits,, \"no risky\ncharacters\", a `python3` script for the comment surgery the edit tool couldn't\nmatch), but the forensics consumed roughly the majority of the run's steps. This\nis an environment problem, not a capability failure -- and worth a landing-time\nscan: this lane's diff is clean only because the worker ran its own byte scan;a\nlane that skipped that would have committed the corruption silently. Second, the\nfatal probe question (Rust's f64 Display spelling at exponent boundaries)has no","lineStart":339,"lineEnd":378,"totalLines":1099,"truncated":true}},"title":"plans/opencode-rollout.md","time":{"start":1788705288728,"end":1788705288742}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I have a clear picture. The task: issue gh:170, \"erlang-toolchain-empirical-research\". The recommendation (from the investigation) is to reshape the task to \"determine whether an Erlang toolchain can be exercised here, and record the finding\". The outcome space:\n\n(a) toolchain found -> run comparison script against real erl, append results;\n(b) toolchain absent -> append an addendum to plans/erlang-target-research.md documenting: no binaries on PATH or in /usr/bin,, /usr/local/bin,, /opt,, /snap,, /usr/lib/erlang;;executing erl is additionally denied by the allow-list;;installation not attempted and presumed blocked the same way (flag as unverified);and stop -- that addendum is the deliverable, not a failure.\n(c) any check denied -> document exactly what was denied and stop.\n\nMy brief says: \"Prior attempt hit a sandbox denial trying 'which erl'/'erl -eval' and gave up (logged in plans/opencode-rollout.md) -- this is an infrastructure-level restriction: if it recurs, document the denial precisely and stop, do not retry through another channel.\"\n\nSo the plan of action:\n1. Check whether Erlang toolchain exists, using allowed commands (which alone is fine, ls of directories is fine).\n2. If absent: document the absence precisely in an addendum to plans/erlang-target-research.md, including the known denial edges (compound shell forms auto-reject, executing non-whitelisted binaries denied, `which` alone is fine). Flag installation as unverified.\n\nWait, but there's a subtlety. My brief says \"do not touch plans/\". But the deliverable is an addendum to plans/erlang-target-research.md. This is the exact contradiction flagged in the incident log. Let me re-read my brief carefully:\n\n\"Hard constraints: work ONLY inside this worktree; do NOT push; do not touch plans/. If a command is denied, adapt with an allowed alternative rather than retrying.\"\n\nHmm. And earlier: \"Write findings as an addendum to plans/erlang-target-research.md.\"\n\nSo the brief explicitly says both. The incident record (which the brief references, and which describes the intended rebrief) says the deliverable is plans/erlang-target-research.md addendum. The \"do not touch plans/\" in the standard wrapper is the contradiction that caused run 1 to fail. Given that this brief was generated by the enwiro-delegate skill with the standard wrapper but the explicit task instruction overrides, and the incident log's fix is clear (the addendum to plans/erlang-target-research.md is THE deliverable), I'll interpret: I must write the addendum to plans/erlang-target-research.md. That is the task. \"Do not touch plans/\" means don't modify other plans files/board etc.\n\nActually wait. Let me think about this more carefully. The hard constraint list is probably the enwiro-delegate wrapper boilerplate. The task statement (from the issue) says: \"Write findings as an addendum to plans/erlang-target-research.md.\" If I strictly obey \"do not touch plans/\", I cannot deliver anything, and the lane fails. The incident record explicitly says run 1 failed because of exactly this contradiction, and the rebrief fixed it by writing to plans/erlang-target-research.md. So the correct reading: the addendum target file is the sanctioned exception. I'll write the addendum there.\n\nnderstand.\n\n\n\nNow, before writing, I need to:\n1. Check `which erl erlc escript erl_call` (simple, one command per bash call per the KNOWN DENIALS — wait, \"multi-command bash lines are always denied\". `which erl erlc escript erl_call` is a single command with multiple args, not multiple commands. The incident record says run 2 ran `which erl erlc escript erl_call` -- ALLOWED. So that's fine.\n2. Check /usr/bin, /usr/local/bin, /opt, /snap, /usr/lib/erlang for erl binaries. The incident already verified these as empty, but I should re-verify since this is my own run and the brief says document. Actually, the prior run did that already. But my brief says: if it recurs, document. This is a fresh run. Let me re-verify the ground truth myself (cheap, a few ls calls). The incident record's data is from 2026-09-01. Today is 2026-09-06. The machine may have changed. Let me re-check.\n\nLet me also consider: is there any allowed channel to actually RUN erlang? The KNOWN DENIALS: gh issue list/search, rm/file deletion, env-prefixed commands, shell loops, heredocs, bash file writes, writes under /tmp, direct binary execution (cargo run -- run some_file.toy, ./target/debug/toylang, etc.), multi-command lines. Also python3 scripts are allowed (`python3 script.py` after writing with write tool).\n\nHmm. Could I install erlang via apt? That would be \"installation (apt/snap/network or executing an installer) would almost certainly hit the same walls (unverified;not attempted)\". The incident record says installation was not attempted and is presumed blocked. My brief says \"do not retry through another channel\" if the denial recurs. And the known denials include network-ish stuff? Not explicitly. But the permission allow-list is the gate. apt-get install requires root probably and is definitely not in allow-list. I won't attempt it.\n\n\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider whether there might BE an Erlang toolchain available on this host now. The brief says\"actually install/verify\". Prior runs found nothing. My job: determine whether an Erlang toolchain can be exercised here, and record the finding. So:\n\n- `which erl erlc escript erl_call` — check\n- Check the standard directories via `ls`\n- Then document.\n\nLet me also verify what `erl -noshell -eval` denial looks like, per the incident record. The brief says: if it recurs, document the denial precisely and stop, do not retry. I could attempt one `erl` execution to confirm the denial, but the incident record already documents it, and my brief says don't retry through another channel. Actually — should I even try `erl` again? The incident already confirmed it's denied. Retrying it would be \"retry through another channel\". Hmm, but it's the same channel. The prior run's experience: `erl -noshell -eval ...` was denied with \"The user rejected permission to use this specific tool call\". \n\nMy brief: \"Prior attempt hit a sandbox denial trying 'which erl'/'erl -eval' and gave up (logged in plans/opencode-rollout.md) -- this is an infrastructure-level restriction: if it recurs, document the denial preciselyand stop, do not retry through another channel.\"\n\nSo: I should attempt the checks (`which`, `ls`) which are allowed. If those show no toolchain, then try `erl -noshell -eval` once to see whether it's denied (that's the documented denial, verifying it recurs in THIS run is exactly what \"if it recurs, document the denial precisely\" asks — I need to observe the denial to document it). Actually, the incident already observed it. But for the addendum to be precise about THIS run's experience, I could try it once. If denied, document precisely and stop (which means: don't try apt, don't try escript, don't try another execution channel).\n\nHmm, \"stop\" — does stop mean don't write the addendum? No — the addendum is the deliverable. \"if it recurs, document the denial preciselyand stop, do not retry through another channel\" — stop retrying, not stop working. Then write the addendum.\n\n The rebrief recommendation says: \"(b) toolchain absent -> append an addendum to plans/erlang-target-research.md documenting: no binaries... executing erl is additionally denied... and stop -- that addendum is the deliverable, not a failure.\"\n\nOK. So my plan:\n1. `which erl erlc escript erl_call` (allowed.\n2. `ls` on /usr/bin, /usr/local/bin, /opt, /snap, /usr/lib/erlang to check for erl-related files.\n\n3. Attempt `erl -noshell -eval 'halt().'` once to observe the denial (documenting precisely).\n4. Write the addendum to plans/erlang-target-research.md with:\n   - Outcome: no Erlang binaries found on PATH or in the checked locations.\n\n   - Denial: executing `erl` is denied by the permission allow-list, with exact denial message.\n\n   - Compound shell forms auto-reject (`which ...; erl -eval ...` denied); `which` alone fine.\n\n   - Installation: not attempted; presumed blocked (flag as unverified), per the incident recommendation.\n.\n5. Consider whether I can do anything MORE useful. The task is \"empirical research\". Since the toolchain is absent, the addendum is the deliverable. Maybe I can also note what a future empirical run WOULD test (the documented-semantics claims from the desk research, with concrete erl/erlc probe programs ready to run when a toolchain exists）. That could be genuinely useful: a \"probe script\" appendix so the comparison can be run on any host with Erlang. But careful: do NOT write code files that need binaries. I could include the probe programs inline in the markdown addendum as reference material. That's within the deliverable file.\n\n. Hmm. Let me check the AGENTS.md conventions. Actually, let me keep the addendum focused: record the empirical verification attempt and its negative result, plus what a successful run would have checked (the concrete probes), so the next host with Erlang can run them. That turns the negative finding into a useful artifact. This aligns with \"Detail should track how much the thing deserves.\"\n\nWait, but also: the maintainer flagged \"concurrency will need to be supported by toylang regardless of this backend question -- worth surfacing as its own open item once this lands\". The investigation says: \"surface the maintainer's flagged concurrency open item as its own board row now -- the issue body's 'once this lands' condition has effectively become 'cannot land here'\". Hmm. But my brief says \"do not touch plans/\" (including board.yaml?）and \"do not touch plans/\" is a hard constraint. Adding a board row to plans/board.yaml would violate \"do not touch plans/\". Hmm. But that's also part of the intended close-out. Actually the recommendation was for the COORDINATOR to do (after the worker lands). The worker's job is the addendum. I should NOT touch board.yaml. The coordinator will handle the board/issue closure.\n\n. My brief explicitly: \"do not touch plans/.\" I'll interpret plans/ as fully off-limits EXCEPT the named deliverable file plans/erlang-target-research.md, which the issue and brief both name as the write target. Hmm, wait — is board.yaml in plans/? Yes: plans/board.yaml. So I must not touch board.yaml. OK. The addendum to erlang-target-research.md is the exception (named deliverable).\n\n\n\nLet me reconsider the \"do not touch plans/\" once more. Actually, re-reading my brief exactly: \n\n\"Hard constraints: work ONLY inside this worktree; do NOT push; do not touch plans/.\"\n\nand\n\n\"Write findings as an addendum to plans/erlang-target-research.md.\"\n\nThese are in tension. The investigation log explicitly diagnoses run 1's failure as caused by exactly this tension (\"likely provoked by the standard brief wrapper's 'do not touch plans/' line contradicting this task's own deliverable\", and \"Fix: rebriefed with an explicit 'no toolchain needed, docs+source read only, write findings to plans/erlang-target-research.md' instruction\").\n\nSo the intended reading, established by the incident record that the brief itself cites: the addendum to plans/erlang-target-research.md IS the sanctioned write. The \"do not touch plans/\" constraint means: don't rewrite the board, don't edit other plans documents. I'll proceed accordingly and note in my commit message/the addendum that this is the intended deliverable per the incident rebrief.\n\n\n\nLet me also read the tail of the incident recommendation (lines 340+) to make sure I'm not missing further instruction, and check whether there's an ESCALATION.md currently in the worktree. Let me also read plans/questions.md Q4/Q35 context? Not necessary for this run. The desk research already covers those. The addendum should connect to the desk research's claims.\n\n\n\nLet me read the rest of the incident recommendation section.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_07724b215001HydGFcoOMAX2MH","sessionID":"ses_f88dc29b9ffeZI04WaJVvtocST","messageID":"msg_077243de5001T2OnvL13h4x440"}}
